From 6444c8adf5f80424107815841bd94cc476a3df65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 01:58:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop the identity disc offering Google sign-in on the new tab
 page

The optional toolbar button on the new tab page became a Google avatar
described as "Signed out. Opens options to sign in.", and tapping it opened a
bottom sheet titled "Sign in to Chrome" carrying the Chrome logo.

Upstream builds that avatar twice. AdaptiveToolbarUiCoordinator chooses between
them on SigninFeatureMap.sSigninLevelUpButton: enabled gives the dedicated
signin button, disabled registers IdentityDiscController as the first button
data provider, where it wins the optional slot on any new tab page. Patch 0020
covers the first branch alone, by pinning that button's properties off.

The flag is FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT, but the field trial testing study
ProfileDiscOnAllPages enables it on Android. Every earlier build therefore
took the branch 0020 covers, and no avatar appeared. Setting
disable_fieldtrial_testing_config for the release build returned the flag to
its code default and uncovered the other branch.

Registering only the adaptive controller settles both branches, so the outcome
no longer depends on the field trial. Registration is the suppression point
rather than the button itself because the bottom bar reads these same providers
through patch 0101, and because a controller that is never registered leaves
nothing watching profile data for a button that is never drawn.

The profile infrastructure below it is untouched. IdentityDiscController still
compiles, and ProfileManager, ProfileAttributesStorage and ProfileResolver are
unchanged.

Sign-in was not reachable behind the sheet either. The tree registers no
AccountManagerDelegate, so NullAccountManagerDelegate reported an empty account
list and returned no add-account intent, and the sheet's button fell through to
Android's own accounts screen.
